<h2>Indian frozen seafood for export</h2>
<p>India is one of the world's largest seafood exporters, and the business divides into two quite different operations: farmed shrimp — overwhelmingly vannamei from Andhra Pradesh and neighbouring aquaculture belts — and wild-caught marine fish and cephalopods landed along both coasts.</p>

<p>We supply frozen vannamei and black tiger shrimp, ribbonfish, Indian mackerel, squid and cuttlefish, pomfret and sardines, processed near the landing and farming centres and shipped from Visakhapatnam, Chennai, Kochi and Nhava Sheva.</p>

<h2>Cold chain is the whole business</h2>
<p>Frozen seafood arrives in the condition its cold chain allowed. Product is frozen and held at −18°C or below, unbroken, from processing through to your cold store. We can supply reefer temperature logs and data-logger records on request — and we'd encourage asking for them, from us or any supplier.</p>

<h2>Compare quotes on the same basis</h2>
<p>Seafood, and shrimp especially, is where quotes most often look comparable but aren't. Three things have to match:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Product form</strong> — HOSO, HLSO, PD, PUD, PDTO or cooked. Yields differ substantially.</li>
  <li><strong>Count size</strong> — pieces per pound. 16/20 and 41/50 are different products.</li>
  <li><strong>Glaze and net weight</strong> — a price on gross weight with 20% glaze is roughly 20% more expensive than the same number on net weight. Always confirm which basis you're being quoted.</li>
</ul>
<p>See our <a href="/products/fish-seafood/frozen-vannamei-shrimp/">frozen vannamei shrimp page</a> for the full specification framework.</p>

<h2>Testing and regulatory scrutiny</h2>
<p>Seafood is among the most closely regulated products in international food trade. The USA, EU and Japan all apply antibiotic residue controls on farmed shrimp, and rejections are costly for both sides. We arrange residue and microbiological testing on request, and {{ site.inspectionStatement | lower }}</p>
